  "query_preview": "..High pressure remains centered across New Mexico \nearly this morning. The high drifts to the west today through \nTuesday before becoming centered over Arizona. As we move through \nthe rest of the week, the high starts to sink further to the south \nand east. \n\nToday and Tuesday...A few showers and isolated storms continue early \nthis morning, mainly across Yavapai County, in response to outflows \nfrom storms across the valley overnight. Precipitation with this \nactivity is light and not expected to be very impactful, other than \nincreasing cloud cover through daybreak. Going through the rest of \ntoday, we see monsoon moisture remaining in place with another \nfairly active afternoon/evening across the region. Best chances will \nbe across the higher terrain and across western portions of the \nforecast area as moisture is advected up from the Gulf of California \nin the continued southerly flow. Storm motions remain on the slow \nside which will keep flash flooding as the main hazard for the day. \nAs the high pushes back to the west we will lose some of that good \nmoisture advection into the region and storm chances will start to \ndecrease. Expect storms to develop mainly across the higher terrain \nfor Tuesday.\n\nWednesday through Friday...As the high becomes centered over \nArizona, we see the moisture start to get cut off as subsidence wins \nout with drier conditions expected. Best chances for any storms will \nremain mainly across the higher terrain with the highest chances \ntowards the White Mountains. The other factor of the strengthening \nhigh will be the increasing temperatures, which will be hot across \nthe region. With highs reaching back into the 100s and heat risk \nincreasing, we have issued an Extreme Heat Warning for the lower \nelevations of the Grand Canyon and Yavapai County, for areas below \n4000 feet, in effect for Wednesday through Friday.\n\nSaturday and Sunday...Models show the high elongating and sliding to \nthe east of the region by the weekend. This shift may help to usher \nmoisture back into our area with better chances for showers and \nthunderstorms returning along with slightly cooler temperatures.",
